1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,792
Thirty-nine minutes after takeoff, this Boeing triple-seven vanished from every radar on Earth.

2
00:00:05,792 --> 00:00:11,252
Two hundred and thirty-nine people were on board. It has never been found.

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:16,383
The last thing anyone on the ground ever heard was utterly routine.

4
00:00:16,383 --> 00:00:20,367
A final handoff to Vietnam, and one calm reply from the cockpit.

5
00:00:21,499 --> 00:00:24,596
Good night. Malaysian three seven zero.

6
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:32,199
Two minutes later, the transponder switched off. No mayday. No wreckage on the water.

7
00:00:32,199 --> 00:00:37,279
No bodies. A wide-body airliner and everyone on it, simply gone.

8
00:00:38,399 --> 00:00:43,925
What followed became the largest and most expensive search in the history of aviation.

9
00:00:43,925 --> 00:00:49,450
More than two hundred thousand square kilometres of deep ocean floor, mapped and scanned.

10
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:53,002
And to this day, it has found almost nothing.

11
00:00:54,133 --> 00:00:59,185
This is the true story of M-H three seven zero. Not the conspiracy theories.

12
00:00:59,185 --> 00:01:07,847
Everything the evidence actually proves, every serious theory that tries to fill the gaps, and the exact line where what we know runs out.

13
00:01:08,966 --> 00:01:12,937
M-H three seven zero. The plane that vanished from Earth.

14
00:01:14,066 --> 00:01:17,540
Chapter 1. The last flight out of Kuala Lumpur.

15
00:01:18,666 --> 00:01:27,362
Just after midnight on the 8th of March, 2014, passengers filed onto Malaysia Airlines Flight three seven zero at Kuala Lumpur International Airport.

16
00:01:27,362 --> 00:01:33,033
An ordinary overnight flight to Beijing, due to land at half past six that morning.

17
00:01:34,166 --> 00:01:37,612
The aircraft was a Boeing triple-seven-two-hundred. Registration nine-M-M-R-O.

18
00:01:37,612 --> 00:01:44,934
Twelve years old, with a clean maintenance record and more than fifty-three thousand hours in the air.

19
00:01:44,934 --> 00:01:52,687
It was one of the safest airliners ever built, operated by an airline with a solid safety history.

20
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:58,750
In the left seat was the captain, Zaharie Ahmad Shah, fifty-three years old.

21
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:09,341
A veteran with over eighteen thousand flying hours, a Malaysia Airlines instructor, and a man so passionate about aviation that he had built a flight simulator at home.

22
00:02:09,341 --> 00:02:12,746
Remember that detail. We will come back to it.

23
00:02:13,866 --> 00:02:17,076
Beside him, First Officer Fariq Abdul Hamid, twenty-seven.

24
00:02:17,076 --> 00:02:24,299
A young pilot with around two thousand seven hundred hours, still completing his conversion training onto the triple-seven.

25
00:02:24,299 --> 00:02:28,312
This was one of his final training flights before qualifying.

26
00:02:29,433 --> 00:02:33,383
Behind them, two hundred and twenty-seven passengers from fourteen nations.

27
00:02:33,383 --> 00:02:36,148
The largest group by far was Chinese.

28
00:02:36,148 --> 00:02:45,629
There were engineers, a delegation of calligraphers, parents flying home to their children, and a flight attendant said to be weeks from her wedding.

29
00:02:46,766 --> 00:02:52,659
In the cargo hold sat something that would later fuel a hundred theories.

30
00:02:52,659 --> 00:02:56,738
More than four tonnes of mangosteens, a tropical fruit.

31
00:02:56,738 --> 00:03:01,724
And around two hundred kilograms of lithium-ion batteries. Both perfectly legal.

32
00:03:01,724 --> 00:03:03,537
Both, later, examined closely.

33
00:03:04,666 --> 00:03:16,191
At forty-one minutes past midnight, Flight three seven zero lifted off and turned north-east, climbing to its cruising altitude of thirty-five thousand feet over the South China Sea.

34
00:03:16,191 --> 00:03:19,895
A calm, clear night. Nothing about it was unusual.

35
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:26,555
For thirty-eight minutes, everything was normal. The crew made routine radio calls.

36
00:03:26,555 --> 00:03:34,839
Then the aircraft approached a waypoint called IGARI, the invisible line where Malaysian airspace ends and Vietnam's begins.

37
00:03:34,839 --> 00:03:36,680
A standard handover point.

38
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:42,868
At nineteen minutes past one in the morning, Kuala Lumpur radar passed the flight along.

39
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,338
Malaysian three seven zero, contact Ho Chi Minh one two zero decimal niner. Good night.

40
00:03:51,466 --> 00:03:54,477
Good night. Malaysian three seven zero.

41
00:03:55,600 --> 00:04:02,436
It was a perfectly normal sign-off. But Flight three seven zero never called Ho Chi Minh.

42
00:04:02,436 --> 00:04:07,564
It never checked in with Vietnam. It never called anyone, anywhere, again.

43
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:18,961
At one twenty-one, the transponder — the box that broadcasts the aircraft's identity, altitude and position to the whole aviation world — went dark.

44
00:04:18,961 --> 00:04:24,947
On every civilian screen from Malaysia to Vietnam, Flight three seven zero simply disappeared.

45
00:04:26,066 --> 00:04:33,662
For four hours, air traffic controllers in two countries would argue about whose airspace it was in, each assuming the other had it.

46
00:04:33,662 --> 00:04:39,937
By the time anyone raised the alarm, the aircraft had been missing, and unwatched, for most of the night.

47
00:04:41,066 --> 00:04:43,417
Chapter 2. The turn.

48
00:04:44,566 --> 00:04:52,208
For hours, everyone believed M-H three seven zero had crashed into the South China Sea, somewhere on its planned route north.

49
00:04:52,208 --> 00:04:55,483
Ships and aircraft rushed to that stretch of water.

50
00:04:55,483 --> 00:05:00,213
They were searching the wrong ocean, on the wrong side of the country.

51
00:05:01,333 --> 00:05:06,657
Because the Malaysian military had been watching something the civilian world could not see.

52
00:05:06,657 --> 00:05:09,699
Military primary radar does not need a transponder.

53
00:05:09,699 --> 00:05:13,503
It bounces a signal off raw metal in the sky.

54
00:05:13,503 --> 00:05:17,686
And that night, it had recorded something that made no sense.

55
00:05:18,833 --> 00:05:23,519
Seconds after it vanished from civilian screens, Flight three seven zero turned.

56
00:05:23,519 --> 00:05:25,081
Not a slow drift.

57
00:05:25,081 --> 00:05:34,062
A hard, deliberate turn to the left, almost doubling back on itself, heading straight back across the Malay Peninsula it had just left.

58
00:05:35,199 --> 00:05:41,305
It flew back over the country. The radar tracked it passing close to the island of Penang.

59
00:05:41,305 --> 00:05:49,567
Then it turned again, up the Strait of Malacca, out towards the open Indian Ocean, away from every airport it could have reached.

60
00:05:50,699 --> 00:05:57,837
The military radar even suggested wild changes in altitude, climbs and descents of thousands of feet.

61
00:05:57,837 --> 00:06:04,529
Investigators later treated those altitude readings with caution, because primary radar is unreliable for height.

62
00:06:04,529 --> 00:06:08,098
But the path itself was clear, and chilling.

63
00:06:09,233 --> 00:06:12,535
Because this was not a plane drifting helplessly.

64
00:06:12,535 --> 00:06:19,551
The turns lined up with real navigation waypoints, the kind a pilot enters into a flight computer.

65
00:06:19,551 --> 00:06:25,742
Someone, in that cockpit, was flying a deliberate route. Away from land. Away from help.

66
00:06:26,866 --> 00:06:33,288
A Malaysian military radar operator watched the unidentified blip cross the country in the middle of the night.

67
00:06:33,288 --> 00:06:34,715
No fighters were scrambled.

68
00:06:34,715 --> 00:06:40,423
In the confusion, an airliner flew across an entire nation's airspace, and no one stopped it.

69
00:06:41,566 --> 00:06:51,566
At two twenty-two in the morning, a military radar station caught the aircraft one final time, roughly two hundred nautical miles north-west of Penang, out over the Andaman Sea.

70
00:06:51,566 --> 00:06:55,359
Then it flew beyond the reach of any radar on Earth.

71
00:06:56,500 --> 00:07:00,066
And there, for the world, the trail went cold.

72
00:07:00,066 --> 00:07:07,993
A Boeing triple-seven had turned itself around, crossed a country, and slipped off the last screen that could see it.

73
00:07:07,993 --> 00:07:10,370
Flying into the dark, towards nothing.

74
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:14,425
Chapter 3. The only witness left.

75
00:07:15,566 --> 00:07:22,031
With the transponder off and the radar behind it, Flight three seven zero should have been truly invisible.

76
00:07:22,031 --> 00:07:24,905
Untrackable. Lost the instant it left the coast.

77
00:07:24,905 --> 00:07:29,933
In almost any other year, that would have been the end of the story.

78
00:07:31,066 --> 00:07:35,205
But something nobody had planned for was still switched on.

79
00:07:35,205 --> 00:07:44,310
High above the Indian Ocean, a single British communications satellite, operated by a company called Inmarsat, was quietly connected to the aircraft.

80
00:07:45,433 --> 00:07:48,520
The aircraft's satellite terminal was still powered.

81
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:59,103
And roughly once an hour, the ground station sent out an automatic electronic ping, a handshake, asking, in effect: are you still logged on?

82
00:07:59,103 --> 00:08:03,954
Each hour, in the dark, Flight three seven zero answered. Yes.

83
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:09,763
These handshakes carried no GPS, no coordinates, no position at all.

84
00:08:09,763 --> 00:08:15,274
Just a machine confirming it was there. To almost anyone, they were meaningless.

85
00:08:15,274 --> 00:08:19,938
But Inmarsat's engineers realised the raw signals held a hidden fingerprint.

86
00:08:21,066 --> 00:08:27,168
The time each ping took to travel to the aircraft and back gave its distance from the satellite.

87
00:08:27,168 --> 00:08:30,219
Plotted on a globe, each handshake became an arc.

88
00:08:30,219 --> 00:08:35,982
A vast curved line, thousands of kilometres long, that the aircraft had to be sitting somewhere along.

89
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:38,870
There were seven of them.

90
00:08:38,870 --> 00:08:44,183
And the last, the seventh arc, is where this entire story would come to rest.

91
00:08:44,183 --> 00:08:49,849
But there was a problem. The arc curved both to the north and to the south.

92
00:08:49,849 --> 00:08:52,329
The aircraft could be on either half.

93
00:08:53,466 --> 00:08:58,504
North led back over land, over Asia, towards places with radar and people.

94
00:08:58,504 --> 00:09:01,993
South led into the empty bottom of the world.

95
00:09:01,993 --> 00:09:07,418
To choose between them, the engineers reached for something almost never used this way.

96
00:09:07,418 --> 00:09:13,619
A tiny shift in the frequency of each signal, caused by the satellite's own slight motion.

97
00:09:13,619 --> 00:09:14,782
The Doppler effect.

98
00:09:15,899 --> 00:09:20,484
The pattern of those frequency shifts fit only one path.

99
00:09:20,484 --> 00:09:23,693
Not north, towards land and rescue. South.

100
00:09:23,693 --> 00:09:30,110
Straight into the emptiest, deepest, most remote stretch of ocean on the entire planet.

101
00:09:31,233 --> 00:09:34,198
It was a staggering piece of detective work.

102
00:09:34,198 --> 00:09:44,578
Boeing, the American and British investigators, and Inmarsat had turned a communications satellite that was never designed to track anything into the only witness to a vanished airliner.

103
00:09:44,578 --> 00:09:46,802
It had never been done before.

104
00:09:47,933 --> 00:09:52,919
But the same analysis delivered a devastating catch. An arc is not a dot.

105
00:09:52,919 --> 00:10:00,755
The relevant stretch of the seventh arc ran for well over a thousand kilometres, above water up to six thousand metres deep.

106
00:10:00,755 --> 00:10:06,454
It told them roughly where the aircraft had run out of sky. Not where it lay.

107
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:13,338
And there was one more clue, buried in the data, that still gives investigators chills.

108
00:10:13,338 --> 00:10:20,225
At two twenty-five in the morning, three minutes after it left radar, the satellite terminal did something strange.

109
00:10:20,225 --> 00:10:25,963
It logged back on. As if its power had been cut, and then deliberately restored.

110
00:10:27,100 --> 00:10:32,138
Something on that aircraft turned a system off, and then, minutes later, back on.

111
00:10:32,138 --> 00:10:36,097
Machines do not do that on their own for no reason.

112
00:10:36,097 --> 00:10:43,295
It is one of the small, unexplained facts that make M-H three seven zero so hard to let go of.

113
00:10:44,433 --> 00:10:47,280
Chapter 4. Seven hours into nowhere.

114
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:52,057
Piece the timeline together and a chilling picture forms.

115
00:10:52,057 --> 00:10:59,777
After that last radar contact, the aircraft made a final major turn to the south, and then flew on.

116
00:10:59,777 --> 00:11:04,654
In a long, straight, steady line. Deep into the southern Indian Ocean.

117
00:11:04,654 --> 00:11:06,686
For nearly six more hours.

118
00:11:07,833 --> 00:11:16,163
Six hours of a fully functioning aircraft, tracking steadily away from any coastline, any airport, any ship, any hope of rescue.

119
00:11:16,163 --> 00:11:22,906
And here the great debate of M-H three seven zero begins. Who, or what, was flying it?

120
00:11:24,033 --> 00:11:30,090
One possibility: no one was flying it at all. A cabin starved of oxygen.

121
00:11:30,090 --> 00:11:36,580
A slow decompression, or a fire that consumed the air, leaving everyone on board unconscious.

122
00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:44,800
The aircraft flying on autopilot like a ghost, carrying the sleeping and the dead, until its fuel ran dry.

123
00:11:45,933 --> 00:11:47,666
The other possibility is darker.

124
00:11:47,666 --> 00:11:53,212
A conscious hand on the controls the entire way, holding a heading chosen for one purpose.

125
00:11:53,212 --> 00:12:00,144
To fly the aircraft to the one part of the ocean where it would be almost impossible to ever find.

126
00:12:01,266 --> 00:12:04,015
The evidence cannot yet settle which it was.

127
00:12:04,015 --> 00:12:08,138
But it can tell us, almost to the minute, how it ended.

128
00:12:08,138 --> 00:12:14,667
At eight eleven that morning, the aircraft answered its seventh and second-to-last handshake, far out over the southern ocean.

129
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:19,189
Then, silence where the eighth should have been.

130
00:12:19,189 --> 00:12:25,544
The engines had almost certainly flamed out, one after another, as the tanks finally emptied.

131
00:12:25,544 --> 00:12:29,356
The aircraft was gliding now, powerless, above the sea.

132
00:12:30,500 --> 00:12:35,150
Eight minutes later, at eight nineteen, there was one last, partial signal.

133
00:12:35,150 --> 00:12:44,452
The kind a system sends when its power has just been restored, most likely by a backup generator kicking in as the aircraft descended.

134
00:12:44,452 --> 00:12:47,166
A dying machine, sending one final word.

135
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:53,960
And the frequency of that very last transmission told investigators something terrible.

136
00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:57,734
The aircraft was descending. Fast. And getting faster.

137
00:12:57,734 --> 00:13:04,339
A rate of descent, in the final moments, far steeper than any normal flight.

138
00:13:05,466 --> 00:13:08,068
Then the seventh arc went quiet forever.

139
00:13:08,068 --> 00:13:16,245
Somewhere below it, in the dark, M-H three seven zero met the surface of one of the most violent oceans on Earth.

140
00:13:16,245 --> 00:13:18,475
And the sea closed over it.

141
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:23,230
Chapter 5. The ocean gives back pieces.

142
00:13:24,366 --> 00:13:30,047
For sixteen months, the ocean gave nothing back. Not a seat cushion. Not a life vest.

143
00:13:30,047 --> 00:13:31,822
Not a single confirmed fragment.

144
00:13:31,822 --> 00:13:41,764
The search moved to the deep seabed beneath the arc, and much of the world quietly began to believe M-H three seven zero would never be seen again.

145
00:13:42,899 --> 00:13:54,777
Then, in July 2015, more than three thousand kilometres from the search zone, a man cleaning a beach on the French island of Réunion, off the coast of Africa, waded into the surf.

146
00:13:54,777 --> 00:13:59,096
Tangled in the tide was a barnacle-covered slab of an aircraft wing.

147
00:14:00,233 --> 00:14:05,353
It was a flaperon. A control surface from the trailing edge of a wing.

148
00:14:05,353 --> 00:14:11,204
A part number, stamped into the metal, traced it beyond any doubt to a Boeing triple-seven.

149
00:14:11,204 --> 00:14:15,227
And only one triple-seven on the entire planet was unaccounted for.

150
00:14:16,366 --> 00:14:21,419
It was the first physical proof of what the satellite data had argued for a year.

151
00:14:21,419 --> 00:14:24,893
M-H three seven zero had come down in the Indian Ocean.

152
00:14:24,893 --> 00:14:30,577
And its wreckage had spent a year drifting, on the great ocean currents, all the way to Africa.

153
00:14:31,700 --> 00:14:33,644
The barnacles told their own story.

154
00:14:33,644 --> 00:14:44,660
Marine biologists studied the species growing on the flaperon, and the chemistry of their shells, to estimate how long it had been at sea and the temperatures of the water it had drifted through.

155
00:14:44,660 --> 00:14:46,929
The ocean itself had kept a record.

156
00:14:48,066 --> 00:14:54,224
And then more pieces came. In Mozambique. In South Africa. In Mauritius. In Tanzania.

157
00:14:54,224 --> 00:14:55,104
In Madagascar.

158
00:14:55,104 --> 00:15:04,340
A scattered trail of debris washing up along the entire western edge of the Indian Ocean, over the following two years.

159
00:15:05,466 --> 00:15:09,652
Many were found not by officials, but by one man.

160
00:15:09,652 --> 00:15:20,952
An American named Blaine Gibson, who became obsessed with the mystery, travelled the beaches of Africa himself, and personally recovered piece after piece, handing them to investigators.

161
00:15:20,952 --> 00:15:24,300
An ordinary man, doing what governments could not.

162
00:15:25,433 --> 00:15:29,645
Oceanographers ran the currents backwards, modelling years of drift in reverse.

163
00:15:29,645 --> 00:15:35,771
The pieces were consistent with an origin near the seventh arc, in the southern Indian Ocean.

164
00:15:35,771 --> 00:15:38,834
Exactly where the satellite had said to look.

165
00:15:39,966 --> 00:15:43,361
Some of the debris was worse than sad.

166
00:15:43,361 --> 00:15:48,029
Investigators identified pieces from the cabin interior, from inside the aircraft.

167
00:15:48,029 --> 00:15:56,940
For interior panels to be torn out and scattered into the sea, the aircraft had almost certainly broken apart on impact.

168
00:15:56,940 --> 00:15:57,364
Violently.

169
00:15:58,500 --> 00:16:03,315
And a study of a wing flap delivered the most unsettling technical clue of all.

170
00:16:03,315 --> 00:16:06,525
Investigators concluded it had not been extended for a landing.

171
00:16:06,525 --> 00:16:10,698
Nobody had set the aircraft up to touch down gently on the water.

172
00:16:11,833 --> 00:16:17,086
In plain terms: at the very end, no one was trying to save it.

173
00:16:17,086 --> 00:16:22,339
M-H three seven zero most likely hit the ocean fast, and steep, and hard.

174
00:16:22,339 --> 00:16:28,342
Which points away from a gentle, piloted ditching, and towards a high-speed dive into the sea.

175
00:16:29,466 --> 00:16:32,235
Chapter 6. Two hundred and thirty-nine.

176
00:16:33,366 --> 00:16:40,028
It is easy, in a mystery this vast, to lose the number at the centre of it.

177
00:16:40,028 --> 00:16:41,987
Two hundred and thirty-nine people.

178
00:16:41,987 --> 00:16:46,297
Twelve crew, and two hundred and twenty-seven passengers, from fourteen countries.

179
00:16:47,433 --> 00:16:53,545
The largest group, by far, was Chinese. One hundred and fifty-three of them.

180
00:16:53,545 --> 00:16:59,658
There were also Malaysians, Indonesians, Australians, Indians, and citizens of nine other nations.

181
00:16:59,658 --> 00:17:04,360
A cross-section of the world, on an ordinary night flight.

182
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:17,244
Among them were artists and engineers, a couple flying home to their two young children, workers returning from contracts abroad, and a stewardess who colleagues said was counting down to her wedding.

183
00:17:17,244 --> 00:17:20,181
Whole lives, and whole families, boarded that aircraft.

184
00:17:21,299 --> 00:17:24,954
Early on, one detail set off global alarm.

185
00:17:24,954 --> 00:17:28,151
Two passengers had boarded using stolen passports.

186
00:17:28,151 --> 00:17:32,262
One Austrian, one Italian, both reported missing in Thailand.

187
00:17:32,262 --> 00:17:36,372
For a few days, the world braced for terrorism.

188
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:41,031
But investigators quickly identified them. Two young Iranian men.

189
00:17:41,031 --> 00:17:49,664
The evidence pointed not to a plot, but to migrants using false documents to try to reach a new life in Europe.

190
00:17:49,664 --> 00:17:52,804
No link to the disappearance was ever found.

191
00:17:52,804 --> 00:17:55,550
It was a heartbreaking coincidence, nothing more.

192
00:17:56,700 --> 00:18:00,592
For the families, the horror took a specific, cruel shape.

193
00:18:00,592 --> 00:18:06,432
There was no crash site to visit. No remains to bury. No coffin to lower.

194
00:18:06,432 --> 00:18:12,661
Just a phone that stopped being answered, and an airline counter with no answers to give.

195
00:18:13,799 --> 00:18:19,176
In Beijing, relatives gathered in a hotel and waited for news that never came.

196
00:18:19,176 --> 00:18:26,090
Some grew so desperate, and so angry at the lack of answers, that the grief spilled into protest.

197
00:18:26,090 --> 00:18:31,851
They had lost their families twice. Once to the ocean, and again to the silence.

198
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:40,282
In 2015, with no wreckage found, Malaysia formally declared the disappearance an accident, and all aboard presumed lost.

199
00:18:40,282 --> 00:18:45,137
It was a legal necessity, so families could begin to claim compensation.

200
00:18:45,137 --> 00:18:51,206
To the relatives, it felt like being asked to bury people who had simply vanished.

201
00:18:52,333 --> 00:18:59,063
Twelve years later, many of those families are still waiting. Not for a body. For a fact.

202
00:18:59,063 --> 00:19:05,002
For someone to finally tell them, with certainty, what happened to the people they loved.

203
00:19:06,133 --> 00:19:09,921
Chapter 7. The theories, weighed by evidence.

204
00:19:11,066 --> 00:19:13,974
When the facts run out, theories rush in.

205
00:19:13,974 --> 00:19:19,790
M-H three seven zero has attracted more of them than almost any event in modern history.

206
00:19:19,790 --> 00:19:23,061
So let's do what the internet almost never does.

207
00:19:23,061 --> 00:19:28,150
Take the serious ones, one by one, and weigh them honestly against the evidence.

208
00:19:29,299 --> 00:19:31,256
Theory one. A catastrophic accident.

209
00:19:31,256 --> 00:19:40,645
A fire, perhaps sparked by those lithium batteries in the hold, or a sudden loss of cabin pressure, that overwhelmed and disabled the crew.

210
00:19:40,645 --> 00:19:45,730
The aircraft then flew on as a ghost until the fuel ran out.

211
00:19:46,866 --> 00:19:51,055
The case for it is simple. It needs no villain.

212
00:19:51,055 --> 00:19:56,500
A crippling emergency could explain the silence and the long, straight, final flight.

213
00:19:56,500 --> 00:19:59,851
The case against it is just as strong.

214
00:19:59,851 --> 00:20:10,742
It struggles to explain the careful turn-back, the navigation along real waypoints, and the transponder switching off at the one perfect blind spot between two countries.

215
00:20:11,866 --> 00:20:17,794
A fire also tends to leave a trail. Frantic radio calls. Erratic, dying flight.

216
00:20:17,794 --> 00:20:25,416
What the radar showed instead was controlled, purposeful flying, for a long time after the aircraft went silent.

217
00:20:25,416 --> 00:20:30,073
That is very hard to square with a burning, crippled jet.

218
00:20:31,199 --> 00:20:34,741
Theory two. Hijacking, by someone other than the pilots.

219
00:20:34,741 --> 00:20:41,430
A takeover of the cockpit, a diversion, a plan that somehow went wrong out over the ocean.

220
00:20:41,430 --> 00:20:43,790
It cannot be completely ruled out.

221
00:20:44,933 --> 00:20:49,989
But the case against it is heavy. No group ever claimed responsibility.

222
00:20:49,989 --> 00:20:54,624
No demands were ever made. No ransom, no manifesto, no cause.

223
00:20:54,624 --> 00:21:02,630
And no trace of a struggle, or of any hijacker, has ever surfaced in the wreckage or the investigation.

224
00:21:02,630 --> 00:21:07,686
A hijacking with no hijacker, and no reason, is hard to sustain.

225
00:21:08,833 --> 00:21:13,554
Theory three. And this is the one investigators keep circling back to.

226
00:21:13,554 --> 00:21:15,522
That the diversion was deliberate.

227
00:21:15,522 --> 00:21:21,424
Carried out by someone who understood that aircraft intimately, and who wanted it to disappear.

228
00:21:22,566 --> 00:21:27,110
The case for it is deeply uncomfortable. The turn-back was flown, not drifted.

229
00:21:27,110 --> 00:21:32,354
The systems that made the aircraft visible went dark in a sequence that looks chosen.

230
00:21:32,354 --> 00:21:33,752
The satellite unit rebooted.

231
00:21:33,752 --> 00:21:41,792
And the plane was steered towards the most remote ocean on the planet, as if by someone who did not want it found.

232
00:21:42,933 --> 00:21:45,297
And then there is the simulator.

233
00:21:45,297 --> 00:21:49,237
Investigators examined the flight simulator the captain kept at home.

234
00:21:49,237 --> 00:21:53,571
On its hard drive, they recovered fragments of a saved route.

235
00:21:53,571 --> 00:21:59,088
A path that ran out over the southern Indian Ocean, ending in the sea.

236
00:21:59,088 --> 00:22:01,846
Broadly, the direction the real aircraft went.

237
00:22:02,966 --> 00:22:08,846
This is exactly where honesty matters most, because a real man's name is attached to it.

238
00:22:08,846 --> 00:22:12,889
That simulator held thousands of data points from years of use.

239
00:22:12,889 --> 00:22:18,402
The official investigation stated the recovered route could not be conclusively linked to the flight.

240
00:22:18,402 --> 00:22:24,282
It is a chilling coincidence. It is not, on its own, proof of what he did.

241
00:22:25,399 --> 00:22:31,236
Captain Zaharie was, by the accounts of those who knew him, an experienced and respected pilot.

242
00:22:31,236 --> 00:22:38,167
No official investigation has ever proven that he, or the first officer, or anyone else, deliberately downed this aircraft.

243
00:22:38,167 --> 00:22:45,827
To declare his guilt, about a man who cannot defend himself, would be to claim something the evidence does not support.

244
00:22:46,966 --> 00:22:51,532
And what about the wild theories? That it was shot down and covered up.

245
00:22:51,532 --> 00:22:55,445
That it was secretly landed at a military base like Diego Garcia.

246
00:22:55,445 --> 00:22:57,728
That it was stolen, or spirited away.

247
00:22:58,866 --> 00:23:00,770
The problem is the evidence.

248
00:23:00,770 --> 00:23:09,530
The Inmarsat handshakes and the debris drifting onto African beaches both point, independently, to the same lonely patch of the southern Indian Ocean.

249
00:23:09,530 --> 00:23:19,051
A landing at a secret base, or a cover-up, has to explain away real wreckage from the real aircraft, washed up by the real sea.

250
00:23:19,051 --> 00:23:20,574
None of them can.

251
00:23:21,699 --> 00:23:26,850
So here is the honest verdict, the one the official investigation itself reached.

252
00:23:26,850 --> 00:23:32,396
Malaysia's final report, released in 2018, was blunt. It could not determine the cause.

253
00:23:32,396 --> 00:23:38,339
It could not rule out that the aircraft had been turned by a third party.

254
00:23:38,339 --> 00:23:43,093
And it could neither convict nor exonerate the men in that cockpit.

255
00:23:44,233 --> 00:23:50,313
That is the truth of M-H three seven zero. Not that we have no idea what happened.

256
00:23:50,313 --> 00:23:52,817
The evidence leans, uncomfortably, in one direction.

257
00:23:52,817 --> 00:23:58,182
But the one thing no one has ever been able to do is prove it.

258
00:23:59,299 --> 00:24:02,381
Chapter 8. The greatest search on Earth.

259
00:24:03,499 --> 00:24:09,021
Finding M-H three seven zero became an obsession, and an engineering campaign unlike anything ever attempted.

260
00:24:09,021 --> 00:24:18,337
The target: a strip of seabed the size of a country, up to six kilometres beneath the surface of one of the roughest oceans on the planet.

261
00:24:19,466 --> 00:24:24,256
First, they had to map a seafloor no human had ever seen.

262
00:24:24,256 --> 00:24:28,247
And what they found down there was a hidden world.

263
00:24:28,247 --> 00:24:36,628
A landscape of underwater mountains, sheer cliffs, and volcanoes, some taller than anything on dry land, buried in total, crushing darkness.

264
00:24:37,766 --> 00:24:40,494
Then they lowered their machines into it.

265
00:24:40,494 --> 00:24:43,611
Ships dragging sonar sleds on cables kilometres long.

266
00:24:43,611 --> 00:24:51,794
Autonomous submarines, flying just above the seabed for hours at a time, listening for the shape of wreckage in the black.

267
00:24:52,933 --> 00:24:58,726
The vessels had names that became familiar to the families. Fugro Equator. Discovery.

268
00:24:58,726 --> 00:24:59,617
GO Phoenix.

269
00:24:59,617 --> 00:25:08,084
They worked the Southern Ocean through brutal weather, in a search coordinated by Australia, on behalf of the world.

270
00:25:09,233 --> 00:25:15,577
That first great effort scanned around one hundred and twenty thousand square kilometres over nearly three years.

271
00:25:15,577 --> 00:25:21,175
It cost close to two hundred million dollars. And in early 2017, it was suspended.

272
00:25:21,175 --> 00:25:27,519
It had found shipwrecks, and a stunning map of the deep. It had not found the plane.

273
00:25:28,666 --> 00:25:31,103
Scientists went back to the drift data.

274
00:25:31,103 --> 00:25:38,762
A study by Australian researchers refined the most likely resting place to a specific stretch of the arc, around thirty-five degrees south.

275
00:25:38,762 --> 00:25:42,244
An area that the first search had not fully covered.

276
00:25:42,244 --> 00:25:47,814
The plane, they argued, might have been just beyond the edge of where they had looked.

277
00:25:48,933 --> 00:25:51,885
Then a private company entered the story.

278
00:25:51,885 --> 00:25:58,632
Ocean Infinity, a firm with a fleet of robotic submarines, made an extraordinary offer to Malaysia.

279
00:25:58,632 --> 00:26:04,957
They would search at their own expense. If they failed, Malaysia would owe them nothing.

280
00:26:04,957 --> 00:26:06,644
No find, no fee.

281
00:26:07,766 --> 00:26:16,271
In 2018, they swept tens of thousands more square kilometres, faster than anyone had ever searched the deep ocean before.

282
00:26:16,271 --> 00:26:19,672
Their robots found centuries-old shipwrecks on the seabed.

283
00:26:19,672 --> 00:26:23,074
They did not find M-H three seven zero.

284
00:26:24,199 --> 00:26:27,261
For years after, the search went quiet.

285
00:26:27,261 --> 00:26:34,696
Amateur investigators kept working the data from their bedrooms, some proposing new locations using experimental radio techniques.

286
00:26:34,696 --> 00:26:39,507
None have been confirmed. The official hunt seemed to be over.

287
00:26:40,633 --> 00:26:44,060
And then, in 2025, Ocean Infinity came back.

288
00:26:44,060 --> 00:26:48,344
Better technology, a refined target zone, and the same deal.

289
00:26:48,344 --> 00:26:55,627
Malaysia agreed to pay a reward of seventy million dollars, but only if they actually found it.

290
00:26:56,766 --> 00:27:03,044
They searched through the southern summer, the only window when that ocean is calm enough to work.

291
00:27:03,044 --> 00:27:06,737
And on the twenty-third of January, 2026, they stopped again.

292
00:27:06,737 --> 00:27:15,231
After more than a hundred and forty thousand square kilometres, the result was the same word the world had heard for twelve years.

293
00:27:15,231 --> 00:27:15,600
Nothing.

294
00:27:16,733 --> 00:27:23,741
A private company had spent its own money hunting the loneliest ocean on Earth, and come home empty.

295
00:27:23,741 --> 00:27:26,077
And yet, they refused to quit.

296
00:27:26,077 --> 00:27:33,086
In 2026, Malaysia extended the agreement, out to the middle of 2027, to search the ground that remains.

297
00:27:34,233 --> 00:27:35,305
So understand this.

298
00:27:35,305 --> 00:27:41,384
As you are watching, the search for M-H three seven zero is not history. It is happening.

299
00:27:41,384 --> 00:27:51,395
Right now, somewhere in the southern Indian Ocean, robotic submarines are still flying through the dark, listening for a plane that has hidden itself for over a decade.

300
00:27:52,533 --> 00:27:56,739
Chapter 9. What it changed. What it left behind.

301
00:27:57,866 --> 00:28:05,403
It seems impossible, in a world where a phone can be tracked across the planet in seconds, that a passenger jet could still simply vanish.

302
00:28:05,403 --> 00:28:08,116
M-H three seven zero exposed exactly how it happened.

303
00:28:08,116 --> 00:28:12,939
And it forced the world to make sure it could never happen the same way again.

304
00:28:14,066 --> 00:28:18,055
Because of this flight, the rules of global aviation changed.

305
00:28:18,055 --> 00:28:22,443
Airliners are now required to report their position far more often.

306
00:28:22,443 --> 00:28:27,629
And new aircraft must carry systems that track themselves automatically in a crisis.

307
00:28:27,629 --> 00:28:33,214
So that no airliner can ever again fly for seven silent hours, completely unseen.

308
00:28:34,333 --> 00:28:37,391
But rules are cold comfort to the families.

309
00:28:37,391 --> 00:28:43,888
For them, M-H three seven zero is not a case study, or a change to a regulation.

310
00:28:43,888 --> 00:28:50,386
It is a father who left for Beijing and never arrived. A daughter whose seat stayed empty.

311
00:28:50,386 --> 00:28:56,119
A question that twelve years, and hundreds of millions of dollars, have still not answered.

312
00:28:57,266 --> 00:28:59,574
They formed groups. They pushed governments.

313
00:28:59,574 --> 00:29:03,420
They refused to let the world move on and forget.

314
00:29:03,420 --> 00:29:08,420
And because of them, the search has never been allowed to truly die.

315
00:29:09,566 --> 00:29:13,728
M-H three seven zero remains the rarest thing in modern aviation.

316
00:29:13,728 --> 00:29:15,620
A disaster with no ending.

317
00:29:15,620 --> 00:29:21,295
A plane that took two hundred and thirty-nine people and, so far, has kept them.

318
00:29:22,433 --> 00:29:24,544
The ocean still holds the answer.

319
00:29:24,544 --> 00:29:29,470
And every day, in the dark, machines are still out there, listening for it.

320
00:29:29,470 --> 00:29:38,268
One day, a robot flying over a ridge four kilometres down may light up a shape that has waited over a decade to be found.

321
00:29:38,268 --> 00:29:40,379
Until then, the story stays open.

322
00:29:41,499 --> 00:29:44,963
M-H three seven zero is the plane we could not find.

323
00:29:44,963 --> 00:29:49,056
But even the crashes we do find can hide answers just as disturbing.

324
00:29:49,056 --> 00:29:56,299
Air France four four seven fell for three and a half minutes into the Atlantic, with its pilots awake the entire way down.

325
00:29:56,299 --> 00:29:59,132
And what its recorders revealed will stay with you.

326
00:30:00,266 --> 00:30:02,643
That breakdown is on the channel now.

327
00:30:02,643 --> 00:30:07,058
And if unsolved mysteries like this one grip you, subscribe to Black Box.

328
00:30:07,058 --> 00:30:13,510
We take the biggest catastrophes in history apart, piece by piece, to find the truth hiding in the details.

329
00:30:13,510 --> 00:30:16,227
Because the truth is always in the details.

